      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "In order to evaluate the effects of formalin concentration and inhalational anesthetics in formalin tests, we injected 5 concentrations of formalin into awake and anesthetized rats and investigated their behavior and the c-fos immunoreactivity of their spinal cords."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Sixty adult male Sprague-Dawley rats were divided into 10 experimental groups. Each rat was either manually restrained or subdued through several minutes of 2% to 2.5% halothane inhalation, and then 1 of 5 concentrations of 0%, 5%, 10%, 27%, or 100% formalin (0%, 1.85%, 3.7%, 10%, or 37% formaldehyde solutions) was injected into its rear paw. Nociceptive behaviors were checked for 1 hour, 6 times for each 5-minute period. Two hours after the formalin injections, the rats were killed and c-fos immunoreactivity was measured."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Typical responses were observed in the 5% and 10% formalin (1.85% and 3.7% formaldehyde) groups, and nociceptive behaviors were lower in the 27% and 100% formalin (10% and 37% formaldehyde) groups. The number of c-fos-positive cells increased as the formalin concentration increased. Halothane inhalation affected the results of both the behavior and the c-fos immunoreactivity, especially in the 10% formalin (3.7% formaldehyde) group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "It is desirable to describe both formalin and formaldehyde concentrations simultaneously and to do formalin tests without inhalational anesthetics."
        }
      ]},
